<title>GERMANY: Mercedes details 9-mo car, truck sales.</title>
<headline>Mercedes details 9-mo car, truck sales.</headline>
<dateline>STUTTGART, Germany 1996-11-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>Nine months to September 30.</p>
<p>(in millions of marks unless stated)</p>
<p>    Revenues				     56,900  vs 52,587</p>
<p>Passenger cars</p>
<p>    -Revenues				    33,953  vs 29,312</p>
<p>    -Production (units)			484,865 vs 442,345</p>
<p>    -Sales (units)			     481,323 vs 426,969</p>
<p>Commercial vehicles</p>
<p>    -Revenues				    22,947  vs 23,275</p>
<p>    -Production (units)			246,084 vs 250,452</p>
<p>    -Sales (units)			     248,602 vs 236,188</p>
<p>NOTE - The full name of the German car and truck making group is Mercedes-Benz AG. The company said it overcame a restrained automotive market with new models and a &quot;definite boost&quot; in productivity. Mercedes said car sales climbed 17 percent in Germany, 45 percent in Japan and 20 percent in the U.S. In the commercial sector, Daimler sold 105,000 vans worldwide, an increase of 29 percent. New registrations of Mercedes trucks over six tonnes were up five percent to 21,700 units in western Europe outside Germany. In Germany, a weak market resulted in an 11 percent drop to 23,000 units, Mercedes said. Mercedes added its new heavy-truck Actros was well received by westernn European customers.</p>
<p>--Scott Miller, Frankfurt Newsroom + 49 69 756525</p>
</text>
